<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dingan</h1><p class="st-meta">938 CE – 986 CE (approx.) · capital Yalu valley</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small successor state of Balhae in the Yalu valley, which resisted the Khitan for half a century and sought alliance with Song China.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/balhae">Balhae</a> in 926, groups of Balhae people in the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu</a> river valley, beyond effective Khitan control, formed their own state. Its beginnings are obscure; it is first recorded in the 930s and 970s, with a royal house sometimes linked to the Yeol family.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Known almost entirely from Chinese and Korean chronicles, Dingan presented itself as heir to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/balhae">Balhae</a>. In 970 its king Yeol Man-hwa (Lie Wanhua) sent envoys to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>, and in 981 King O Hyeon-myeong (Wu Xuanming) proposed joint action against the Khitan, but no help came.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shengzong-of-liao">Emperor Shengzong of Liao</a> sent a campaign in 985 that destroyed the kingdom by 986. Its people were deported or fled, many to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo</a>, which absorbed numerous <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/balhae">Balhae</a> refugees in this period.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 938 CE – 986 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:78.37%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>938 CE – 986 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yalu valley</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yeol Man-hwa, O Hyeon-myeong</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jeongan, Kingdom of Dingan, Jeongan-guk, Ding'an, Later Balhae</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=962&amp;focus=dingan" aria-label="Dingan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7421 990 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dingan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8093 871h0M7866 1006h0M7901 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7741 1246h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7741 1246h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=962&amp;focus=dingan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
